# WordPress Theme Generation Prompt

Create a complete, polished WordPress website theme using the business, content, visual design, page, component, form, accessibility, and conversion requirements defined below.

The completed website must feel intentionally designed for this specific business. It must not resemble a generic template with only the company name and text replaced.

Do not create unfinished sections, empty navigation items, broken links, missing imagery, nonfunctional controls, duplicated page layouts, or pages that use the same design with only their text changed.

## 01. Business Identity

### Business Name

Northstar Websites

### Short Business Description

Northstar Codeworks is a senior software development company that builds custom web applications, internal tools, API integrations, and automation systems for growing service businesses and B2B teams.

The company helps founders and operations leaders replace fragile spreadsheets, disconnected SaaS tools, and manual processes with reliable software that is easier to run, maintain, and scale.

### Industry and Category

Northstar Codeworks is a software development company focused on AI automation, custom software, custom web applications, internal tools, administration portals, API integrations, workflow automation, business dashboards, analytics systems, workflow optimization, legacy system modernization, technical discovery, and software architecture.

### Target Audience

The website should speak directly to small businesses, growing service businesses, operations teams, B2B companies, ecommerce teams, founders, business owners, operations leaders, technical buyers, and companies that need custom software or AI-assisted systems.

These visitors may currently rely on fragile spreadsheets, manual data entry, repetitive administrative work, disconnected SaaS platforms, duplicated information, outdated software, processes that are difficult to scale, or systems that no longer fit the way their business operates.

### Primary Conversion Goal

The primary conversion goal is to encourage qualified visitors to book a consultation for a custom software project, web application, internal tool, automation project, AI system, API integration, technical discovery engagement, or legacy modernization project.

The primary call to action should be **Book a Consultation**.

### Secondary Conversion Goals

Secondary conversion goals include encouraging visitors to explore the service pages, read case studies, understand the development process, request a project planning guide, join a practical software operations newsletter, learn about technical discovery, review available engagement models, and understand ongoing support and maintenance options.

## 02. CSS Requirements

### Overall CSS Direction

The website should use clean white and off-white backgrounds, dark navy text, bright accent colors, rounded cards, soft shadows, large typography, strong call-to-action sections, responsive grids, mobile-first layouts, accessible focus states, consistent spacing, and controlled content widths.

The styling must remain consistent across all pages and components.

### CSS Custom Properties

Use CSS custom properties to establish a reusable design system.

```css
:root {
    --nyt-color-dark: #111827;
    --nyt-color-muted: #6b7280;
    --nyt-color-light: #f8fafc;
    --nyt-color-surface: #ffffff;
    --nyt-color-accent: #2563eb;
    --nyt-color-accent-2: #7c3aed;
    --nyt-color-border: #e5e7eb;
    --nyt-radius-sm: 0.5rem;
    --nyt-radius-md: 1rem;
    --nyt-radius-lg: 1.5rem;
    --nyt-shadow-soft: 0 18px 45px rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.10);
    --nyt-container: 1180px;
}
```

Additional custom properties may be added when they improve consistency across spacing, typography, colors, shadows, responsive behavior, and reusable components.

### Required Styling Coverage

Create complete styling for body content, headings, paragraphs, lists, links, buttons, containers, navigation, dropdowns, mobile navigation, backdrops, footer areas, hero sections, cards, responsive grids, accordions, carousels, portfolio filters, before-and-after placeholders, forms, validation states, comments, search interfaces, archive content, standard pages, service pages, case studies, the 404 page, screen-reader text, skip links, focus states, sticky calls to action, dashboard panels, workflow diagrams, metric panels, and code-inspired interface cards.

### Responsive Requirements

The website must use a mobile-first responsive approach.

Content widths, section spacing, typography, cards, forms, navigation, dashboard visuals, interface panels, and footer columns must respond cleanly across desktop, tablet, and mobile screen sizes.

The completed website must not contain horizontal scrolling, clipped cards, clipped buttons, overflowing menus, oversized headings, overflowing hero graphics, text hidden behind sticky navigation, unstable dropdowns, or layers that appear underneath unrelated page content.

### Interaction States

Interactive elements must have clear default, hover, focus, focus-visible, active, expanded, selected, disabled, validation-error, and validation-success states wherever those states apply.

Focus indicators must remain clearly visible on both light and dark backgrounds.

### Motion and Animation

Animations should support the design without blocking interaction, causing layout shifts, distracting visitors, or reducing readability.

Scroll animations, hero animations, carousel movement, interface transitions, and decorative motion must respect the visitor’s reduced-motion preference.

```css
@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    /* Disable or simplify nonessential motion. */
}
```

Essential information must never depend entirely on animation.

### Accessibility Requirements

Use semantic HTML, proper heading order, visible focus states, sufficient color contrast, readable text sizes, large enough mobile tap targets, keyboard-accessible controls, accessible dropdowns, accessible accordions, proper image alternative text, reduced-motion support, a working skip-to-content link, and screen-reader utility text.

Hidden controls and links must not remain keyboard-focusable.

Use buttons for actions and links for navigation. Do not use noninteractive elements as controls unless they receive complete semantic and keyboard support.

