## 04. Header

### Desktop Header Layout

Create a polished, responsive, accessible header with the logo on the left, the main navigation in the center, and the primary call-to-action button on the far right.

Keep the spacing tight, balanced, and professional.

### Main Navigation

The main navigation should contain Home, Services, Work, Process, Resources, About, and Contact.

The Services navigation area should include Custom Web Applications, Internal Tools, API Integrations, and Automation Systems.

Every navigation item must connect to a real page, meaningful page section, content archive, or relevant destination.

Do not create empty dropdowns or navigation items without a working destination.

### Desktop Dropdown and Panel Behavior

Desktop dropdowns and navigation panels must use solid readable backgrounds, correct positioning, stable layering, keyboard accessibility, clear active states, and visible focus states.

Panels must open and close when their trigger is selected. Only one panel may remain open at a time. Selecting an active trigger must close its panel. Clicking outside the panel or pressing Escape must close the active panel.

A backdrop should appear while a large navigation panel is open. Body scrolling should lock while the panel is active.

The `aria-expanded` state must update accurately. Use `aria-controls` where appropriate. Closed panels must be hidden from assistive technology and must not leave hidden controls available through keyboard navigation.

Where a panel includes a left navigation rail and a right content area, the right-side content should update when a left-rail item receives pointer hover or keyboard focus.

All panel content must remain readable and appear above the page content.

### Mobile Header

Create a dedicated mobile navigation drawer rather than placing the full desktop navigation directly into the mobile page layout.

The mobile header must include a hamburger open button, accessible navigation drawer, dedicated close button, backdrop, backdrop-click closing, outside-click closing, Escape-key closing, accurate `aria-expanded` behavior, body scroll locking, a solid readable background, a full-width call-to-action button, and protection against horizontal overflow and keyboard traps.

### Mobile Navigation Accordions

Use mobile accordions for Services, About Us, and Blog.

Use direct mobile links for Work and Contact.

Where the completed site uses the labels About and Resources, connect the related mobile items to those actual destinations.

Closed accordions must not leave hidden links available through keyboard navigation.

### Header Accessibility

The header must use semantic navigation markup, descriptive control labels, keyboard-operable controls, accurate expanded and collapsed states, logical focus order, visible focus states, and no hover-only functionality.

Opening and closing the mobile drawer or desktop navigation panels must not create keyboard traps.

