## 11. Forms

### Form Administration

Create realistic, polished, responsive form experiences.

Form submissions must be viewable from a WordPress administration area named **Forms**.

The administrator must be able to export all form submissions, submissions across all forms, or submissions from one selected form.

The forms must not require an external plugin dependency.

### Contact Form Fields

The contact form should contain Name, Email, Phone, Company or Organization, and Message fields.

### Quote and Consultation Form Fields

The quote or consultation form should contain Name, Email, Phone, Project Type, Business Type, Current Website URL where applicable, Goals, Timeline, and Budget Range fields.

### Form Experience

Forms must use visible labels, clear required-field indicators, helpful instructions, appropriate input types, accessible error messages, accessible success messages, server-side validation, input sanitization, submission protection, clear submission states, keyboard accessibility, and responsive field layouts.

Do not use placeholder text as the only label for a form field.

### Form Security

Form handling must include request verification, server-side validation, input sanitization, output escaping, permission checks for administrative areas, safe export handling, direct-access protection, clear failure states, and protection against exposing sensitive information.

### Conversion Elements

Include repeated calls to action where they support the page content, clear buttons above the fold, a contact call to action in the footer, a call to action after major service explanations, trust indicators near forms, a consultation prompt after case studies, a clear call to action on the Process page, relevant calls to action on resource content, and a final conversion section on major pages.

Calls to action should be relevant to the current content rather than repeating the same generic sentence throughout the website.

